tasks.prompt:
A parking garage stores each session as a JSON document with a "zone" (Text), "level" (Int), "duration_minutes" (Int), and "paid" (Bool). The following 4 sessions are given:

session1 = {"zone": "A", "level": 2, "duration_minutes": 95, "paid": true}
session2 = {"zone": "B", "level": 1, "duration_minutes": 140, "paid": false}
session3 = {"zone": "A", "level": 3, "duration_minutes": 60, "paid": true}
session4 = {"zone": "C", "level": 2, "duration_minutes": 180, "paid": false}

Write a program that prints a summary report. Output format, exactly one line per session, in order:

ZONE <zone> | LEVEL <level> | MIN <duration_minutes> | <PAID or UNPAID>

After the session lines, print one final line:

PAID <count> / <total>

where <count> is the number of sessions with paid=true and <total> is the number of sessions.

Example for a single session with zone A, level 2, duration 95, paid true:
ZONE A | LEVEL 2 | MIN 95 | PAID

No trailing spaces on any line.

An aquarium keeper records tank data as a JSON document:

{
  "tanks": {
    "fresh": {
      "A1": {"fish": 12, "plants": 3, "ph": 7.1},
      "A2": {"fish": 8, "plants": 5, "ph": 6.8}
    },
    "marine": {
      "B1": {"fish": 15, "plants": 1, "ph": 8.2},
      "B2": {"fish": 6, "plants": 0, "ph": 8.4}
    }
  }
}

Write a program that walks every leaf in this JSON document and prints each leaf as a full key path (dot-separated from root to leaf), followed by a colon, a space, and the value. Order the output first by the top-level group (fresh before marine, as they appear), then by tank id (A1, A2, B1, B2), then by field name alphabetically (fish, ph, plants).

Output format (exactly 12 lines):
tanks.fresh.A1.fish: 12
tanks.fresh.A1.ph: 7.1
tanks.fresh.A1.plants: 3
tanks.fresh.A2.fish: 8
tanks.fresh.A2.ph: 6.8
tanks.fresh.A2.plants: 5
tanks.marine.B1.fish: 15
tanks.marine.B1.ph: 8.2
tanks.marine.B1.plants: 1
tanks.marine.B2.fish: 6
tanks.marine.B2.ph: 8.4
tanks.marine.B2.plants: 0

Real values are printed with exactly 1 decimal place. No trailing spaces on any line.

A community garden stores soil readings for each plot as a JSON document like {"plot":"P1","ph":6.5,"moisture":42,"sun":"full"}. Below are five plots as JSON documents.

Plot JSON documents:
{"plot":"P1","ph":6.5,"moisture":42,"sun":"full"}
{"plot":"P2","ph":7.1,"moisture":35,"sun":"part"}
{"plot":"P3","ph":5.9,"moisture":51,"sun":"shade"}
{"plot":"P4","ph":6.8,"moisture":44,"sun":"full"}
{"plot":"P5","ph":7.4,"moisture":29,"sun":"part"}

Output one line per plot in the order given, in the exact format:
<plot> pH=<ph, one decimal place> MOIST=<moisture> SUN=<sun>
After all plots, output one line:
ACIDIC COUNT <number of plots with ph strictly less than 6.5>

A print shop tracks ink cartridges for each press as a JSON document like {"press":"R1","inks":[{"color":"Cyan","pct":82},{"color":"Magenta","pct":45}],"jobs":6}. Below are four presses as JSON documents.

Press JSON documents:
{"press":"R1","inks":[{"color":"Cyan","pct":82},{"color":"Magenta","pct":45}],"jobs":6}
{"press":"R2","inks":[{"color":"Cyan","pct":37},{"color":"Yellow","pct":91}],"jobs":4}
{"press":"R3","inks":[{"color":"Black","pct":64},{"color":"Magenta","pct":73}],"jobs":9}
{"press":"R4","inks":[{"color":"Black","pct":22},{"color":"Yellow","pct":58}],"jobs":3}

Output one line per press in the order given, in the exact format:
PRESS <press> | INKS <count of entries in inks array> | LOWEST <smallest pct> | JOBS <jobs>
After all presses, output one line:
LOW INK COLORS <number of individual ink entries whose pct is strictly below 50>

A beekeeper records hive status as a JSON document like {"hive":"H1","queen":"seen","frames":10,"honey_kg":14.5,"mites":3}. Below are five hives as JSON documents.

Hive JSON documents:
{"hive":"H1","queen":"seen","frames":10,"honey_kg":14.5,"mites":3}
{"hive":"H2","queen":"seen","frames":12,"honey_kg":18.2,"mites":1}
{"hive":"H3","queen":"missing","frames":8,"honey_kg":9.7,"mites":6}
{"hive":"H4","queen":"seen","frames":11,"honey_kg":16.8,"mites":4}
{"hive":"H5","queen":"missing","frames":7,"honey_kg":6.3,"mites":8}

Output one line per hive in the order given, in the exact format:
HIVE <hive> | QUEEN <queen> | FRAMES <frames> | HONEY <honey_kg, one decimal place> | MITES <mites>
After all hives, output one line:
NEEDS CHECK <number of hives whose queen is missing OR whose mites are strictly greater than 5>

A marathon timing crew stores runner records as JSON documents like {"bib":"101","name":"Ana","splits":[12.5,13.1,13.4],"dnf":false}. Below are four runners as JSON documents.

Runner JSON documents:
{"bib":"101","name":"Ana","splits":[12.5,13.1,13.4],"dnf":false}
{"bib":"102","name":"Ben","splits":[11.9,12.3],"dnf":true}
{"bib":"103","name":"Cara","splits":[14.2,13.9,14.5,14.1],"dnf":false}
{"bib":"104","name":"Dev","splits":[12.8,13.0,13.2],"dnf":false}

Output one line per runner in the order given, in the exact format:
BIB <bib> | NAME <name> | SPLITS <count of entries in splits array> | TOTAL <sum of all splits, two decimal places> | DNF <dnf>
After all runners, output one line:
FASTEST AVERAGE <the runner with the smallest average split time, printed as their name>

An aquarium stores water test results as a JSON document like {"tank":"A1","tests":[{"param":"Ammonia","value":0.02},{"param":"Nitrite","value":0.0}],"stable":true}. Below are three tanks as JSON documents.

Tank JSON documents:
{"tank":"A1","tests":[{"param":"Ammonia","value":0.02},{"param":"Nitrite","value":0.0},{"param":"Nitrate","value":12}],"stable":true}
{"tank":"A2","tests":[{"param":"Ammonia","value":0.05},{"param":"Nitrite","value":0.01},{"param":"Nitrate","value":8}],"stable":false}
{"tank":"A3","tests":[{"param":"Ammonia","value":0.0},{"param":"Nitrite","value":0.0},{"param":"Nitrate","value":20}],"stable":true}

Output one line per tank in the order given, in the exact format:
TANK <tank> | TESTS <count of entries in tests array> | STABLE <stable> | MAX <the largest value among all tests for this tank, two decimal places>
After all tanks, output one line:
ALERT TANKS <number of tanks whose stable field is false>
